ReXGlue audio: XMA decoder input checks and stream-end fixes, from the WetRecomp SDK fork.

Taken unchanged from nikolaygorb/rexglue-sdk at dba1a70 (WetRecomp V1.6, based on v0.10.1):
src/audio/xma_context.cpp and include/rex/audio/xma/context.h. Nothing else from the fork is
needed. Some of it follows xenia-canary.

v0.10.1 trusts the frame size read from the stream. A read offset that isn't at a frame, or data
that isn't XMA, gives a size under 8 bits, and BitStream::Copy then hands memcpy about 2^61 bytes:
a write far past the decoder's buffer on the PC heap. This is the likely cause of 007: Quantum of
Solace's heap corruption (0xC0000374) as its opening cutscene ends and the audio streams switch.
The fork checks that each frame starts in its packet, is at least a frame header long and lies in
the two packets copied, and otherwise skips to the next packet. It also:
- checks input buffers lie in guest physical memory before reading them;
- stops a kick that makes no progress (stalled read offset, or 1024 passes with no output);
- doesn't abandon a partly consumed frame, and flushes a stream's last frame when input ends;
- rechecks the context is still allocated and enabled under the lock before decoding;
- keeps the write offset at the read offset while starved (stall detectors in some titles).

Same licence as the SDK (BSD-style, see its LICENSE; the file is derived from Xenia).
Credit: nikolaygorb (WetRecomp) for the fork changes.

